# ---------------------------------------------------------------- top-level text
def _summary(ctx):
    return ("Hair + Me's public consultation collects answers; it does not decide anything a visitor can see. Stage 2 replayed it %d times (%d baseline replays, %d experiments that changed one answer, and %d group tests of non-medical answers) and compared every screen and the bag with a pool of baseline visits. "
            "Only a handful of answers do anything visible: sex at birth swaps the men's and women's question blocks; an age under 18 is the single hard stop; two or more goals, a blood-pressure diagnosis and 'pregnant, breastfeeding or likely to conceive' each add one screen; 'no hair loss' skips one; pets add a warning and a 'Better for pet owners' badge on The Capsules; previous treatments and 'Other' reveal a tick-box or a text box; and a gender identity other than man removes one marketing screen from the men's route. "
            "Everything else, including patchy or sudden loss, an active scalp infection, heart disease, phaeochromocytoma, depression, prostate or breast cancer history, a minoxidil allergy, choosing drops over capsules and declining finasteride, leads to exactly the same screens and the same bag: 'Prescription · Personalised treatment · 4 week supply · 12ml', £34.99. "
            "The real engine is a pharmacist prescriber who, after account creation and payment, picks one of 16 compounded formulas (6 capsules, 10 drops). Its published limits are the leaflet contraindications: no finasteride or dutasteride for women; no oral minoxidil with heart disease, phaeochromocytoma or uncontrolled blood pressure; no finasteride or dutasteride with prostate, breast, sexual-function or mental-health history; no drops on an inflamed or infected scalp; only niacinamide drops in pregnancy or breastfeeding. The company says the choice 'isn't automated or AI': a clinician reads the medical history, hair-loss pattern, lifestyle and goals and selects 'the right active ingredients and concentrations'. Its articles call finasteride first-line and dutasteride second-line (more advanced loss, marked crown thinning, or a limited response to finasteride), and point women with PCOS towards once-a-day minoxidil drops. Which strength a patient starts on is not published, and no prescription was seen.") % (ctx['N_S2'], ctx['N_S2_BASE'], ctx['N_S2_OC'], ctx['N_S2_SW'])
